Operator guide

SATGS provides a native station window and the satgs-operator terminal command. Both read the same saved workspace. Use the window for routine configuration and review, and the CLI when you need repeatable inputs, full identifiers or SSH access. The optional browser dashboard is a different application.
Daily quickstart

If the accepted daily application is already selected, leave the selection alone. Selection chooses an installed application; it does not open a window, choose hardware or select a telemetry source. After a separately reviewed update, source that update's launchers/setup_daily.sh with its documented selection name. The accepted native release uses --select ui; this guide candidate uses --select guide. Do not select a candidate after failed acceptance.

On the station's local or remote desktop, open the application menu and click SATGS. The desktop entry declares the Science category. Desktop menus may group that category differently; the precise Pi submenu still needs a direct menu-click observation. In a desktop terminal, the short alternative is:

satgs-operator-gui

No environment activation or versioned installation directory is needed. Inspect the existing station before changing it:

satgs-operator status
satgs-operator docs

The daily launcher resolves its saved workspace, normally /home/admin/satgs/station, from the shared selection. A missing station causes an error, not automatic initialization. For the following practice actions, create a separate station using the hands-on tutorial. Never initialize the daily station merely to replace unknown status fields.

In the practice window, use these steps:

    On Home, inspect Status, then use Reload (Ctrl+R) after another interface saves.
    On Setup, choose guided_arrow, click Preview, review the proposed selection, then click Save. This saves planning intent, not an installation or connection.
    On Location, enter the tutorial's synthetic decimal-degree coordinates and altitude in metres. Preview opens Review. Cancel (Esc) discards that preview; Preview again and Save records the values.
    Close and reopen the same practice workspace. Its saved selection remains.
    On Results, provide the tutorial's local historical TLE CSV and UTC start, then click Calculate. Read the opportunities and Report. A calculation is not a tracking run.
    Use only the separately supplied controlled rehearsal procedure when a software run is needed. Selecting rehearsal on Run does not admit owners. The rehearsal section explains the supported boundary.
    On Results, choose a new report filename and use Export.... Keep the report and workspace. A second export to the same filename fails without replacing it.

Sourced setup changes PATH in that terminal only. A new terminal must find its own command. If needed, use ~/.local/bin/satgs-operator status or ~/.local/bin/satgs-operator-gui; these are the installed daily wrappers, not new aliases. The application menu uses an absolute wrapper path. Shell profiles and global PATH are not changed.

From an SSH-only session, use satgs-operator docs --text and the CLI. A forwarded or unrelated display is not permission to open another desktop session. Do not force DISPLAY=:0. A remote desktop attached to your ordinary graphical login can use the menu just like the local desktop.
Open this guide

satgs-operator docs
satgs-operator --read-docs
satgs-operator docs --text

The first two commands use the same dispatcher. On a usable desktop they send this installed release's local HTML guide to the default viewer. Successful dispatch does not prove that a window rendered. In SSH/headless sessions, or when the viewer is unavailable, fails or times out, readable terminal text is provided instead. --text always prints without a display, Tk, browser or pager. The native window's Help > User Guide opens this same guide and retains edits.

Documentation works before station creation and with missing or invalid station configuration. It does not connect owners, read credentials, start services or change the daily selection. All chapters are bundled offline with the selected release. There is no hosted site, localhost server or source-checkout fallback.
Installation, configuration and observations

Three different questions appear in normal station use. Installation asks which software is present. Configuration records intended components and station values. Observation records what a source actually reported and when. Saving a profile answers the second question only. It does not make the first or third healthy.
Displayed state 	What it means 	Appropriate next action
Selection: No data	No component selection is saved in this workspace.	Inspect the intended deployment, then Preview and Save a supported selection if a change is needed.
No location set.	No effective saved station coordinates are selected.	Enter a deliberate manual position in a practice workspace first; do not infer one from a screenshot or device label.
unknown	This workspace has no evidence establishing that dimension. Installation status is intentionally unknown even when package metadata exists.	Inspect the source and assigned validation record. Unknown is not proof of missing hardware or missing packages.
unqualified calibration or heading	The interface has no admitted qualification for calibration or antenna heading.	Use the separate approved alignment/qualification procedure. Coordinates do not establish alignment.
unconfigured	No canonical component selection is saved.	Choose a supported configuration when authorized.
selected_local	Configuration intent is saved locally.	Review its constraints. This does not establish owner readiness.
critical with transport_pass	A synthetic observation can report critical health while message transport passes.	Keep the dimensions separate; receiving a message does not establish navigation or physical health.

Generation is the saved configuration's sequence, not a count of messages or an installation result. A newly created station starts at generation 1. The observed generation 1 and unconfigured/unknown fields therefore do not overturn a separately reported acceptance PASS.

Home exposes connection, message receipt, fix, freshness, system health, transport check, calibration, heading, installation and configuration separately. The CLI's --format json retains source identities and diagnostic detail omitted from everyday human views. Report/export retain provenance rather than turning unavailable evidence into success.
Choose a configuration

Setup's Profile offers full_msel, guided_arrow and custom. The PTU model in this physical pelican-qpt130 case is QPT130 only. The wider shared product keeps its existing Full M-SEL model contracts, including QPT-200 and QPT-500. This case's power/bench applicability does not remove those wider models.

full_msel describes the established full suite. guided_arrow describes its canonical modular foundation, with explicit local and external responsibilities. custom edits canonical component modes and providers. Modes are install, reuse, external and omit; these are planning choices, not actions that the native window performs. An external provider still needs its independently configured service, identity and contract.

Click Preview to inspect the exact proposed selection and affected plans. Save confirms only that preview. Cancel (Esc) leaves saved configuration unchanged. Full M-SEL's optional local hardware does not declare stable instances. Switching that selection to Custom can therefore produce an unapplied draft with missing identity diagnostics and Save disabled. The displayed intent is retained. Start from Guided Arrow's declared instances, deliberately change selection modes, or provide complete canonical JSON through components-preview --changes. Do not invent hardware identities to silence a diagnostic.

The native application has no installer or service-activation button. The separate deployment planner and legacy Full M-SEL installer are described in the comparison.

