Practice without hardware

This tutorial extends the earlier operator practice workflow with the current window labels and daily launchers. It uses a new private directory, synthetic site coordinates and a historical NOAA 15 TLE. It never uses the daily station as a disposable fixture. None of the steps below starts a service or opens a device.
Inspect the existing station first

From any normal working directory:

satgs-operator status
satgs-operator --format json status

Home > Reload (Ctrl+R) is the matching native action. These commands inspect the already selected workspace. They do not initialize or repair it. If the daily selection or workspace is unavailable, retain the diagnostic and read satgs-operator docs --text; do not issue init against the daily path.
Create a private practice workspace

Use Bash and the daily satgs-operator command already selected by reviewed setup. The following creates a unique parent directory under /tmp; it changes only the task-specific variables shown. Keep the printed path for reopening.

satgs_practice_root=$(mktemp -d /tmp/satgs-guide-practice.XXXXXXXX) || exit 1
satgs_practice_station="$satgs_practice_root/practice station"
printf 'Practice directory: %s\n' "$satgs_practice_root"
satgs-operator --workspace "$satgs_practice_station" init
satgs-operator --workspace "$satgs_practice_station" status
satgs-operator-gui --workspace "$satgs_practice_station" &

The new station starts at generation 1 with no selection and no location. Unknown observations and unqualified calibration/heading are expected. The space in practice station is deliberate; retain the quotes around its path. The final & leaves the graphical window running while this terminal accepts the paired CLI examples. Close that window normally when the exercise is done. The daily selection still points to the original saved workspace. The explicit --workspace changes this invocation only. Keep this terminal open for the remaining examples, or set the two task-specific variables to the exact printed paths in another terminal.

The examples below use 34.0 degrees north, -118.0 degrees east and 100.0 metres, later changing longitude to -117.0. These are synthetic practice values chosen for a repeatable calculation, not a surveyed site or Cole's location. Mean-sea-level reference is explicitly declared for the exercise; no conversion is performed.
Select Guided Arrow and retain returned identities

In Setup choose guided_arrow and QPT130, then Preview. Review shows the previous and proposed selection. Click Save, then Reload to see the saved intent. To perform the same change through the CLI instead, use this sequence once:

satgs_component_preview=$(satgs-operator --workspace "$satgs_practice_station" --format json components-preview --profile guided_arrow) || exit 1
satgs_component_id=$(printf '%s' "$satgs_component_preview" | python3 -c 'import json,sys; print(json.load(sys.stdin)["result"]["preview_id"])') || exit 1
printf '%s\n' "$satgs_component_preview"
satgs-operator --workspace "$satgs_practice_station" apply --preview "$satgs_component_id" --confirm
satgs-operator --workspace "$satgs_practice_station" reload

Review the printed JSON before the apply line. preview_id is the actual value returned by your operation, not a fixed token to copy from this guide. Human output intentionally omits internal IDs, so use --format json before the command when a later command needs an identity. Stop if preview or JSON parsing fails. The GUI and CLI alternatives are the same workflow; do not mistake doing both for a necessary second save.
Cancel a location preview

In Location enter latitude 34, longitude -118, altitude 100, and mean_sea_level. Click Preview, inspect Review, then Cancel (Esc). Home > Reload must still show no effective location. Equivalent CLI:

satgs_location_preview=$(satgs-operator --workspace "$satgs_practice_station" --format json location-preview --latitude-deg 34 --longitude-deg -118 --altitude-m 100 --altitude-reference mean_sea_level) || exit 1
satgs_location_id=$(printf '%s' "$satgs_location_preview" | python3 -c 'import json,sys; print(json.load(sys.stdin)["result"]["preview_id"])') || exit 1
printf '%s\n' "$satgs_location_preview"
satgs-operator --workspace "$satgs_practice_station" cancel --preview "$satgs_location_id"
satgs-operator --workspace "$satgs_practice_station" status

Cancelling preserves saved coordinates and generation. It retains a cancellation artifact, so it is not a promise of zero filesystem writes. Repeating cancel is idempotent. Applying that cancelled preview must return conflict, exit 3.
Save and reopen the manual position

Preview the values again. The previous cancelled identity is not reusable. Review the new proposal, then Save. Through the CLI:

satgs_location_preview=$(satgs-operator --workspace "$satgs_practice_station" --format json location-preview --latitude-deg 34 --longitude-deg -118 --altitude-m 100 --altitude-reference mean_sea_level) || exit 1
satgs_location_id=$(printf '%s' "$satgs_location_preview" | python3 -c 'import json,sys; print(json.load(sys.stdin)["result"]["preview_id"])') || exit 1
printf '%s\n' "$satgs_location_preview"
satgs-operator --workspace "$satgs_practice_station" apply --preview "$satgs_location_id" --confirm
satgs-operator --workspace "$satgs_practice_station" reload

After the one component save and this location save, generation is 3 if you have made no other changes. Status reports manual latitude 34.0, longitude -118.0, altitude 100.0 and declared reference mean_sea_level in JSON. The everyday view may omit technical identity fields. GPS stays unavailable, installation unknown and heading/calibration unqualified.

Close the window normally and run the same quoted GUI command from the first section again, or use Home > Open station... and choose this practice directory. Reload must retain the manual selection. Opening another workspace in a window does not retarget the saved daily launcher selection.
Plan from an explicitly local historical TLE

Create a local CSV with the same explicit historical data used by the bundled example. The shell redirects only into your new private practice directory. It does not require the application's Python interpreter, discover an old installation or fetch a TLE:

satgs_practice_tle="$satgs_practice_root/NOAA 15 historical.csv"
printf '%s\n' 'norad_id,name,tle,date' '25338,NOAA 15,"NOAA 15,1 25338U 98030A   25135.84934273  .00000229  00000+0  11192-3 0  9996,2 25338  98.5367 161.5741 0011424  74.5549 285.6893 14.26965248404873",2025-05-16 02:51:09 UTC' > "$satgs_practice_tle"
satgs-operator --workspace "$satgs_practice_station" --format json plan --start 2025-05-16T03:00:00Z --norad-id 25338 --span-days 1 --elevation-deg 20 --tle-file "$satgs_practice_tle"

The resulting file has this header and one quoted TLE record. If entering it in a text editor instead, preserve both lines and save it at the exact satgs_practice_tle path. Do not replace a real station's satellite catalog.

norad_id,name,tle,date
25338,NOAA 15,"NOAA 15,1 25338U 98030A   25135.84934273  .00000229  00000+0  11192-3 0  9996,2 25338  98.5367 161.5741 0011424  74.5549 285.6893 14.26965248404873",2025-05-16 02:51:09 UTC

Then run the plan line. In the GUI, set Results fields to the same start, NORAD ID 25338, span 1, elevation 20 and full local CSV path, then Calculate. The expected result has source: operator_selected_local_tle, refresh_performed: false, execution_enabled: false, and opportunities from the real orbit calculation. The exact plan ID depends on your workspace identity and revision. No fixed ID in a document can replace it. Keep the historical date visible: this exercise does not demonstrate current orbit freshness.
Compare planning inputs after relocation

In Location change longitude from -118 to -117, leaving latitude 34, altitude 100 and reference unchanged. Preview and Save. CLI equivalent:

satgs_relocation_preview=$(satgs-operator --workspace "$satgs_practice_station" --format json location-preview --latitude-deg 34 --longitude-deg -117 --altitude-m 100 --altitude-reference mean_sea_level) || exit 1
satgs_relocation_id=$(printf '%s' "$satgs_relocation_preview" | python3 -c 'import json,sys; print(json.load(sys.stdin)["result"]["preview_id"])') || exit 1
printf '%s\n' "$satgs_relocation_preview"
satgs-operator --workspace "$satgs_practice_station" apply --preview "$satgs_relocation_id" --confirm
satgs-operator --workspace "$satgs_practice_station" --format json status
satgs-operator --workspace "$satgs_practice_station" --format json plan --start 2025-05-16T03:00:00Z --norad-id 25338 --span-days 1 --elevation-deg 20 --tle-file "$satgs_practice_tle"

The earlier plan is marked revalidation_required: true. The new plan has a different station revision and longitude. TLE bytes and calculation time/span/ mask are unchanged. Compare those inputs before comparing opportunity times. This is a relocation of the planning site, not an antenna alignment command.
Inspect independent observation states

The optional synthetic status exercise is explicit and local:

satgs-operator --workspace "$satgs_practice_station" replay --state critical
satgs-operator --workspace "$satgs_practice_station" --format json status

Home > Reload shows critical system health alongside transport_pass and unqualified heading. JSON identifies synthetic_replay, fixed fixture time and live_hardware_observed: false. The saved manual position does not change. replay --state unknown restores unknown replay dimensions; it does not undo the component or location saves.
Rehearsal and unavailable live controls

The public Run form can freeze and review an input-bound request, but ordinary owner admission remains closed. There is no copy-and-run live or rehearsal run-start command that this guide can supply without the separately reviewed controlled owners and exact inputs. Do not construct broker, serial or device identities from the practice coordinates.

The retained hardware-free daily-support delivery provides this separate procedure, only when that accepted support directory and its recorded runtime environment are present:

"$HOME/Downloads/satgs-daily-support-20261111/rehearse.sh" --accept-trusted-native

That script deliberately targets the retained runtime application, creates its own disposable workspace and records an evidence report. It does not exercise this guide candidate's selected GUI and does not initialize the daily station. Its controlled real-owner boundaries are not a broker or serial-device run. The guide candidate's consolidated acceptance checks documentation, worked examples and Help behavior, not those older runtime scenarios again. Use each accompanying report for its actual software outcomes; do not treat an unexecuted command as a PASS.

For a started controlled run, Run > Cancel run and run-cancel --run-id request local cancellation. Before starting, run-status/run-cancel return not_started, not successful cancellation. Keep software completion, command submission, manager outcome and physical state separate. PTU Connect/Move/Stop remain gated for live use; no tutorial step enables them.
Inspect and export results

satgs-operator --workspace "$satgs_practice_station" report
satgs-operator --workspace "$satgs_practice_station" --format json report
satgs-operator --workspace "$satgs_practice_station" export --output "$satgs_practice_root/practice report.json"

Results > Report and Export... are the equivalent GUI actions. Inspect the position/reference, retained plans, replay provenance and operation results in the JSON. Export must create a private new file. Repeating the exact export returns exit 4 and leaves the existing bytes intact. Choose another filename if you need another snapshot. Keep the practice directory and report; no deletion or cleanup is part of this tutorial.
Deliberate negative checks

These checks belong only to this new practice workspace. Invalid latitude nan must return exit 2 without changing saved coordinates. Applying a valid preview without --confirm must return exit 2. Applying a cancelled or stale preview must return exit 3. A preview made before the relocation becomes stale. Do not weaken these restrictions to make a script proceed.

The complete argument grammar and outcome meanings are in the CLI reference. The original-workflow comparison explains why a local planning exercise does not replace queued RF tracking or legacy result storage.
CLI reference

The public application commands are satgs-operator and satgs-operator-gui. Installed daily wrappers choose the saved application and workspace. They work outside the extraction directory. The direct CLI package entry point used without those wrappers defaults to ./satgs-workspace; the direct GUI without --workspace opens with no station selected. Specify an absolute --workspace when using either directly, instead of confusing those developer behaviors with the shared daily selection.
Command grammar

satgs-operator [--workspace PATH] [--format human|json] COMMAND [OPTIONS]
satgs-operator --read-docs
satgs-operator-gui [--workspace PATH]

Global options precede the command. Arguments are not abbreviated. Quote paths containing spaces. --workspace affects one invocation, not the saved daily selection. Human output uses readable fields and omits low-level IDs and paths. JSON returns the established status and result, or status and error, envelope. Read a returned ID from result; never invent one. JSON errors retain their code and message. Use satgs-operator --help or COMMAND --help for the installed parser's exact option spelling.
Documentation
Form 	Behavior
satgs-operator docs	Opens the bundled HTML with the ordinary local default viewer on a usable desktop; otherwise prints readable terminal text.
satgs-operator --read-docs	Alias of the same docs dispatcher, with the same selection and fallback behavior.
satgs-operator docs --text	Prints the terminal guide directly; no display, Tk, browser or pager.
satgs-operator --format json docs --text	Preserves the CLI JSON envelope, including documentation mode/message/text and technical resource identity.

Docs are available with missing or invalid configuration and before station initialization. SSH sessions use terminal fallback even if an inherited display variable exists. Viewer absence, failure or timeout falls back to text. Exit 0 then means documentation was delivered, not that a window rendered. Missing or unreadable packaged resources return docs_unavailable, exit 4. There is no topic option; docs --topic anything is invalid input, exit 2.
Workspace inspection and creation
Command 	Options 	Effect and matching GUI action
init	--case pelican-qpt130, the default and only supported case	Creates a new private workspace only. Home > New station.... Refuses existing or unsuitable targets rather than resetting them.
status	None	Reads saved station, generation/revision, configuration, manual/effective/replay position and independent status dimensions. Home > Reload (Ctrl+R).
reload	None	Same saved-state read as status. Required after another interface saves.
catalog	None	Reads canonical profiles, components and constraints. Corresponds to Setup's available choices, not installed hardware inventory.
report	None	Reads the redacted report with retained plans and operation results. Results > Report.

status and report do not create or initialize a workspace, contact a broker or construct operational owners. Reports are local evidence, not proof of an active process. Keep credentials and workspace data outside source history.
Component selection

components-preview --profile guided_arrow|full_msel|custom
                   [--changes JSON_PATH] [--ptu-model QPT130]
                   [--expected-revision SHA256]

The profile is required. Model defaults to QPT130; QPT-130 is also accepted by the shared case validator. Other models are rejected for this Pelican case. --changes reads a bounded ordinary UTF-8 JSON file containing either canonical selection fields or a component-to-change mapping. Unknown components, unsupported provider combinations and missing stable hardware instance identities are rejected. The CLI and Setup use the same validator.

For example, {"rf.frontend":"omit"} expresses an explicit component-mode change for a Custom draft. It is not an instruction to uninstall a package or switch off equipment. Complete canonical documents must satisfy their real identity, provider and version contracts. Do not infer those from this one-field example.

Preview returns result.preview_id, previous/proposed values, the expected station revision and affected plans. It saves a preview artifact without applying configuration. Supplying --expected-revision allows a caller to reject a concurrent change explicitly. A revision is the exact saved document's 64-character SHA-256 identity from JSON status, not a generation number.
Manual location

location-preview --latitude-deg NUMBER --longitude-deg NUMBER
                 --altitude-m NUMBER
                 --altitude-reference unspecified|mean_sea_level
                 [--expected-revision SHA256]

All four location inputs are required. Latitude/longitude are decimal degrees, north/east positive. Altitude is metres, not feet. Values must be finite and pass the shared station-location validator; latitude is [-90,90], longitude [-180,180]. The reference is declared, not measured or converted. Location > Preview is equivalent. The returned preview does not move equipment.
Apply or cancel a preview

apply --preview PREVIEW_ID --confirm
cancel --preview PREVIEW_ID

PREVIEW_ID is the exact returned preview identity. Apply requires explicit confirmation after review. Review > Save applies the same operation. Cancel corresponds to Review > Cancel (Esc), leaves station settings unchanged and records cancellation. Repeated successful apply/cancel is idempotent. A cancelled preview, stale revision or conflicting active managed runner cannot be forced through. A successful save can be followed by partial receipt/I/O failure; inspect retained state before retrying.
Numerical pass planning

plan [--start ISO8601_WITH_OFFSET] [--norad-id INTEGER]
     [--span-days NUMBER] [--elevation-deg NUMBER]
     [--tle-file LOCAL_CSV]

Option 	Default 	Units and restriction
--start	2025-05-16T03:00:00Z	Explicit UTC offset required; normalized to UTC. Historical replay default.
--norad-id	25338	Positive integer through 2147483647, present unambiguously in the CSV.
--span-days	1	Finite days from 1/24 through 7.
--elevation-deg	20	Finite degrees from 0 through 90.
--tle-file	Bundled historical NOAA 15 CSV	Bounded ordinary local CSV; no refresh, URL, broker lookup or source modification.

This uses the real numerical orbit calculation and records a plan, with at most 256 opportunities. Results > Calculate is equivalent. Missing saved location, invalid time/units, malformed or mismatched TLEs and missing scientific dependencies are reported. A plan records its input location/reference/revision, TLE hash and epoch, refresh=false and execution_enabled=false. It is not a queue submission or completed tracking run.
Runtime request review and execution

The following is a grammar reference, not a ready-to-run hardware example:

run-preview --plan-id PLAN_ID --deployment-config CONFIG.json
            --runtime-bindings BINDINGS.json --station-config OWNERS.json
            --tle-file LOCAL.csv --duration SECONDS
            [--opportunity INTEGER] [--mode operational|rehearsal]
run-start --run-id REQUEST_ID --confirm
run-status --run-id REQUEST_ID
run-cancel --run-id REQUEST_ID

PLAN_ID is an existing saved plan identity. CONFIG.json is the canonical deployment configuration matching the saved selection. BINDINGS.json is the reviewed runtime-owner binding document. OWNERS.json contains the explicit station/owner settings accepted by the runtime reader. LOCAL.csv must match the exact local TLE used for planning. These are separately supplied private reviewed inputs, not filenames that SATGS creates automatically.

Duration is positive finite seconds that fit the selected opportunity. Opportunity is 1-based and defaults to 1. Mode defaults to operational. Review run freezes all input identities and the saved position/reference; Start run separately confirms. Changes after review require a new request. Ordinary production admission remains empty. rehearsal records intent, not a permission bypass. No valid live owner recipe is supplied by this release.

run-start owns synchronous CLI execution and prints progress on stderr. Use a separate terminal with the same workspace and request ID for status or local cancellation. run-status before start reports not_started. A saved unfinished progress record does not establish a live process. Cancel run does not prove remote cancellation or physical Stop. A finished result is returned on repeated execution of that request rather than silently repeated motion.
Reports, export and replay

export --output NEW_FILE
replay --state STATE

Export writes one new private JSON report and refuses an existing output. The parent directory must already be suitable; - is not an output path. Results > Export... is equivalent. No database or remote-storage action is implied.

Replay is an explicit CLI-only synthetic observation selection. States are unknown, disconnected, silent, no_fix, reported_3d, stale, mismatched, incomplete and critical. Home > Reload displays the saved scenario. It does not overwrite the manual position, connect hardware or make GPS eligible for station selection. Source/time/fix/freshness remain distinct.
PTU command session

bench --configuration BENCH.json

The interactive commands are connect, status, preview PAN TILT, move, stop, disconnect and quit. Pan/tilt are degrees. The same session operations back the PTU page. BENCH.json is an approved private setup document with exact manager, wire-address, serial, protocol, calibration/no-go and trust inputs. Neither a device label nor a guessed USB path supplies those inputs. Current live admission is closed. The guide does not prescribe a live Connect/Move/Stop sequence. Passive cleanup or a returned acknowledgement is not physical Stop.
Exit codes and result meaning
Exit 	Meaning
0	Requested local operation completed, or documentation delivered. Not installation/navigation readiness or physical completion.
2	Invalid arguments, invalid input, unsupported choice, or a missing/malformed station document rejected by the package reader.
3	Conflict, stale/cancelled preview, workspace activity/private-directory/marker admission rejection, unavailable prerequisite, not_started or rejected owner/runtime admission.
4	I/O, calculation, partial local operation, unavailable guide resource or unexpected local failure.
5	Runtime execution failure.
6	Runtime result/progress persistence failure. Keep partial artifacts.
7	Runtime cleanup failure where reported by the runner.
8	Physical state indeterminate. JSON status is INDETERMINATE, not PASS.
130	Local CLI/run cancellation or interruption. Does not establish physical Stop.

For runtime results inspect primary, manager_outcome, software_completed, physical_state, submission details and cleanup/save fields together. The operation's evidence is more specific than a single process exit code.

The daily wrapper validates its own selection, application and workspace before the package runs. Its missing/unsuitable selected-path failures can return exit 1 with a launcher diagnostic rather than the package JSON envelope. Keep the launcher and package failure layers separate. Docs bypasses station validation, not the selected-application identity check.

