Offline documentation build and packaging

Five canonical Markdown chapters under ground_station/docs produce one linked satgs/data/user-guide.html, one user-guide.txt, local user-guide.css and a deterministic user-guide-manifest.json. One unchanged actual accepted native Setup screenshot is packaged as user-guide-setup.png; its caption distinguishes the controlled host capture from Pi and new Help-action evidence. Its source and output bytes are bound in the manifest. The builder supports the small source subset used here: headings, paragraphs, code fences, tables, lists and explicit links. It escapes source text, checks internal links, and requires no external Markdown library. The package build calls it through the existing build_py flow. The compiler stays in source/build tooling, outside runtime imports.

The manifest records relative canonical source names/hashes, selected package version and output hashes. It omits current timestamps and local checkout paths so identical source/version produces identical resources. HTML uses system fonts, local CSS, semantic headings/landmarks, a skip link, visible focus, scrollable tables and print styling. It has no scripts, analytics or remote assets. The terminal edition retains exact code examples, screenshot alternative text and the provenance caption without HTML markup or image bytes.

Installed lookup is relative to the selected satgs package, never current directory, Downloads or a source checkout. The same wheel carries the content opened by CLI and Help. Older installations keep their own guide, if they have one; selecting this release does not search for a newer manual on a moving branch. Documentation-only paths avoid workspace/configuration loaders and optional owner stacks. Default-viewer dispatch uses a known local executable and argument array, never a shell command, BROWSER string, pager or server.
Validation and evidence interpretation

The accompanying candidate acceptance report is the authority for commands actually executed, installed origins, assertions and artifacts. Review these distinct checks: fresh wheel resources outside checkout; all three docs forms; missing/invalid station; wrong working directory; paths with spaces; old/new installed resources; headless/SSH/text; viewer success/failure/timeout; and documentation-only imports with effects denied. A controlled viewer substitution tests dispatch mechanics, not the desktop's rendered content.

The native check must invoke the real Help callback with real widgets, preserve unsaved draft/result/tab state, demonstrate event-loop responsiveness and retain owned cleanup. Content checks compare CLI parser/options and current widget labels, validate internal links/assets and execute worked examples only in new private workspaces under the existing guards. Legacy broker/hardware commands remain source reviewed. Prior Pi/native/telemetry evidence is retained without rerunning unrelated historical suites or attributing its PASS to changed bytes.

If a current capability is implemented but not target-tested, report that exact state. Do not convert an operator-observed PASS into a new independent run, or call a source-reviewed command an executed example. Reports keep software completion, submission and physical state separate.
Update, selection and rollback

Use the candidate's supplied side-by-side setup command and consolidated acceptance command. They reuse retained offline dependency inputs and create separate candidate environments. They do not patch accepted site-packages, move virtual environments, reset the saved station or select a release merely because setup succeeded. Candidate selection is explicit after reviewed acceptance.

If the candidate is rejected, retain the prior selection. If an already selected candidate must be rolled back, use the retained reviewed launcher setup to select the previous accepted identity (ui for the accepted native UI, or the specifically approved runtime identity). Preserve environments, workspaces, partial results and evidence. Rollback is selection, not deletion or a claim of physical stopping. No database migration or workspace schema conversion is introduced by the guide feature.
